course: dictionary
lesson: 14456
title: Parched: dry, thirsty, or roasted
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is right. «The parched fields cracked in the summer heat.» Fields cannot be thirsty, so parched means very dry, because of heat and no rain.
explain: Now read «After the football match, I'm parched!» A person can be parched, and then it means very thirsty. This is informal, so you would say it to friends, not write it in a school essay.
explain: Look at the words around it to decide. «The sun parched the grass» uses parched as a verb, the past form of parch. Try the swap check: put "very dry" or "very thirsty" into the sentence and see which one makes sense.
fact: Parched means very dry, because of heat and lack of rain.
fact: «Parched fields» are fields where the soil has dried out and may crack.
fact: Informal use: «I'm parched!» means I really need a drink.
fact: Parched can also describe food that has been dried or roasted, such as «parched corn».
fact: The verb parch goes: parch, parches, parched, parching.
exercise: «The parched fields cracked in the summer heat.» What does parched mean here? | *Very dry, with no rain | Very loud | Very wet and muddy | Very cold and icy
exercise: Mia says, «I'm parched after running round the park.» What is she telling us? | She is feeling sleepy | She is very cold | *She really needs a drink | She has lost her bag
exercise: «The explorer's lips were parched after three days in the desert.» Find and copy the word that is the past form of the verb parch. | *parched | desert | lips | three
test: «The parched desert traveller staggered on.» What does a parched desert show about the story? | Lots of rain and floods | Music and dancing | Cold and snow | *Heat and danger
test: Which word completes this sentence: «Last summer the sun ___ the grass brown.» | parching | parch | *parched | parches
exam: Ruby says «I'm parched» means she is hungry. Is she right? | No, it means she is cold | Yes, parched always means hungry | Yes, because parched food is always hungry | *No, it means very thirsty, so she needs a drink
